- One citizen gets one vote, most votes win in [[FPTP]]
- Problem 1:
    - Usually a minority wants the winner to win
    - E.g. Belfast south in 2015 24.5% of votes for winner 75.5% for losers collectively
- Problem 2:
    - Inevitable and unavoidable two party system
    - People use [[tactical voting]] and avoid unelectable extremists to avoid wasting their votes
    - They vote for who they hate least instead of who they like the most
    - Eventually this turns into a two party system e.g. most of the U.K. with [[Conservative party]] and [[labour-party]]
- Problem 3:
    - Leaves people who no longer vote for people they like disengage
    - [[Turnout]] reduces in FPTP because people don’t think their vote matters really
- Problem 4:
    - [[Gerrymandering]]
    - Whoever draws the lines of election boundaries has immense power e.g. Florida boundaries
- Problem 5:
    - Third candidates can take votes away from main parties - this means that the main party with voters being taken away won’t be elected
    - Third parties often split the vote and give ideologically opposed parties the win
    - The better a third party does the worse it is for the main party they’re taking votes from
